/.env
# Would otherwise be copied into the image by `COPY . /app` — it holds the
# production database password and the token signing key.
/.env.prod
/.deploy/
# Deploy tooling: it builds the image, it does not belong inside it.
/deploy/
# The whole directory. The service reads none of it — the documents are served
# out of the database, not the filesystem — and it holds the filled-in résumé,
# its metadata and the tailoring notes: contact details, per-bullet figures, and
# a former employer's internal scale and vendor stack. `COPY . /app` would
# otherwise bake all of that into an image pushed to Artifact Registry, where
# anyone with registry read access could pull it back out.
#
# Excluding by directory rather than by filename on purpose: the previous
# per-file list silently stopped covering resume.json and resume.metadata.json
# the moment those were added.
/docs/
.venv/
data/
.idea/
.coverage
.pytest_cache/
htmlcov/
__pycache__/
.ruff_cache/
.git/
.github/
/tests/
# clients/web/index.html itself is wanted in the image — CLIENT_HTML_PATH
# points at /app/clients/web/index.html and GET /client serves it. These two
# are not: they are whatever clients/web/build.sh last left on the developer's
# machine, gitignored and therefore invisible to review, and `COPY . /app`
# would bake them in. A stale dist/ is the worse of the two — it would be
# served to real users as though it were the file in the repository.
/clients/web/dist/
/clients/web/node_modules/

coverage.xml
